  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms have continued in\nnorthwestern portions of the area in response to an upper-level\ndisturbance and approaching cold front. These storms are\nexpected to continue through the afternoon and evening hours,\ngradually drifting south (more to come later). These conditions\nwill allow for a gradient in high temperatures across the area\ntoday. The cold front and associated showers/thunderstorms will\nbring a break from the heat for northwestern portions of the\narea, keeping highs in the 80s. However, the hot and humid \nconditions will continue through today for southern portions of \nthe area, namely along and south of the state line, as highs \nclimb in the upper 90s-low 100s and heat index values approach \n105-108. A Heat Advisory will remain in effect until 7PM \ntonight.\n\nAs the cold front passes through the area this afternoon,\nscattered thunderstorms are expected to develop and drift\nsouthward. A few of these storms this evening could become\nstrong to marginally severe, as ample heating and southerly flow\nsouth of the boundary allow for some instability rebuild (around\n1000-2000 J/kg of CAPE). \n\nNear normal temperatures are expected Wednesday and Thursday in\nthe wake of this cold front, as a cooler drier air mass moves \ninto the area and the upper level pattern flattens out. Highs \nare expected to be in the 80s, with little to no precipitation \nchances in the forecast. Breezy southerly flow may return \nThursday, with gusts up to 20-30 MPH possible during the \nafternoon hours.\n\nThe EFI-SOT (which is a measure of how extreme model guidance is\ncompared to climatology) indicates that above normal \ntemperatures may return to the area Friday (with 60-70% of model\nguidance suggesting above normal temperatures), as strong \nsoutherly flow returns to the area under a slightly more\namplified pattern. However, it will likely not be as hot as \nthis past stretch, as highs are expected to be in the upper 80s \nto mid 90s, compared to climatologically residing in the mid \n80s. However, this heat won't stick around long, as an upper- \nlevel disturbance approaches from the west. Along with the cool-\ndown, this disturbance brings with it the chance of \nshowers/thunderstorms Friday evening through Monday. A few of\nthese storms Friday may be strong to severe.",
